    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nServing large models at low latency and high throughput requires partitioning the model across multiple accelerators. Two dominant sharding strategies are:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a slice of *every* layer, so all devices participate in every layer's computation.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: used in Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models. Each device holds a *disjoint subset of experts* (whole FFN blocks), while attention and other dense layers are typically replicated (or sharded via TP/DP on top).\n\nIn practice, large MoE deployments combine both: TP for attention and shared layers, EP for the expert FFNs, and data parallelism (DP) across replicas of the whole thing. This note focuses on the mechanics of TP and EP in the *inference* (decode/prefill) path: data flow, the collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block with:\n\n- Attention projections: $W_Q, W_K, W_V \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$, output projection $W_O \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times d}$\n- MLP: up/gate projections $W_{up}, W_{gate} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times 4d}$, down projection $W_{down} \\in \\mathbb{R}^{4d \\times d}$\n\nWith TP degree $T$ (world size $T$ devices):\n\n- **Column-parallel (split output dim)**: $W_Q, W_K, W_V$, $W_{up}$, $W",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
